<!-- =========================
RESEARCH INSIGHTS
========================= -->

<section class="section">

<div class="section-heading">

<h2>
Career Intelligence: Concepts Worth Knowing
</h2>

<p>
These ideas are more useful than generic advice because they change how you
evaluate decisions.
</p>

</div>


<div class="research">


<div class="research-card">

<div class="source">
Concept / Human Capital
</div>

<h3>
Skills Can Compound
</h3>

<p>
A capability becomes more valuable when it can combine with other
capabilities. Communication plus analytics, for example, can create a
different opportunity set than either skill alone.
</p>

</div>


<div class="research-card">

<div class="source">
Concept / Signaling
</div>

<h3>
Knowing Is Not the Same as Signaling
</h3>

<p>
A person may possess knowledge without being able to communicate credible
evidence of it. Projects, demonstrations, work samples, and outcomes reduce
the uncertainty others face when evaluating capability.
</p>

</div>


<div class="research-card">

<div class="source">
Concept / Optionality
</div>

<h3>
Some Moves Increase Future Choice
</h3>

<p>
A strategically useful move can create several future paths instead of
locking a person into one narrow route. Transferable skills are particularly
important for this reason.
</p>

</div>


<div class="research-card">

<div class="source">
Concept / Opportunity Cost
</div>

<h3>
Every Choice Uses Scarce Resources
</h3>

<p>
Time spent learning one skill cannot simultaneously be spent developing
another. A good career decision considers what you are giving up, not only
what you are gaining.
</p>

</div>


<div class="research-card">

<div class="source">
Concept / Feedback Loops
</div>

<h3>
Your Search Should Teach You Something
</h3>

<p>
Applications, interviews, projects, and conversations can generate feedback.
If the same strategy repeatedly produces weak results, the process should
change rather than simply increase in volume.
</p>

</div>


<div class="research-card">

<div class="source">
Concept / Portfolio Strategy
</div>

<h3>
One Career Can Contain Multiple Assets
</h3>

<p>
A modern career can contain employment, credentials, projects, relationships,
specialized knowledge, reputation, and independent income-producing
capabilities.
</p>

</div>


</div>

</section>


